---
import MapQuizLayout from '../../layouts/MapQuizLayout.astro';

// Counts and facts come from scripts/build-map-quiz.mjs (world-countries,
// Natural Earth 1:50m, World Bank 2024 population).
const faqs: Array<[string, string]> = [
  ['How many countries are in South America?', 'Twelve: Argentina, Bolivia, Brazil, Chile, Colombia, Ecuador, Guyana, Paraguay, Peru, Suriname, Uruguay and Venezuela. French Guiana and the Falkland Islands are drawn on the map but not asked, because they belong to France and the United Kingdom.'],
  ['Which South American countries are landlocked?', 'Two: Bolivia and Paraguay. Bolivia lost its coast to Chile in the War of the Pacific (1879–1884).'],
  ['What is the smallest country in South America?', 'Suriname, at about 164,000 km² and 630,000 people. Uruguay is the second smallest by area, and the smallest Spanish-speaking country on the continent.'],
  ['Is the South America map quiz good for beginners?', 'Yes. With 12 countries and no tiny islands, the South America map quiz is the shortest continent quiz on the site and a good first full map to finish.'],
  ['Is the South America map quiz free?', 'Yes. Click, Type and Learn modes are free with no account, and your best accuracy and time are saved in your browser.'],
  ['Can I print a map of South America?', 'Yes. The free worksheet numbers all 12 countries, with blank lines for the names and a separate answer key.'],
];
const howTo: Array<[string, string]> = [
  ['Read the name', 'The bar above the map names one South American country at a time.'],
  ['Click it', 'Click or tap it on the map.'],
  ['Learn from misses', 'A wrong click shows which country you hit; three misses reveal the answer.'],
  ['Finish all 12', 'Work through the continent and check your accuracy and time.'],
];
---

<MapQuizLayout
  region="south-america"
  title="South America Map Quiz – All 12 Countries, Free & Printable"
  description="Click all 12 countries on our free South America map quiz, then name them in type mode. Learn mode with capitals and facts, plus a printable worksheet."
  h1="South America Map Quiz"
  sub="Click the country we name — all 12 countries, no sign-up, <strong>free printable worksheet</strong>."
  faqs={faqs}
  gameDescription="A free South America map quiz covering all 12 countries, with click, type and learn modes."
  howTo={howTo}
>
  <h2>How the South America Map Quiz Works</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The South America map quiz names one of the continent’s 12 countries, and you click it on
    the map.</strong> First-try answers turn green and answers after a miss turn amber. After three
    wrong clicks the quiz reveals the right country, and every wrong click tells you which country
    you did click, so a mistake still teaches you something.
  </p>
  <p>
    With only a dozen countries, a full run takes a minute or two, which makes the South America map
    quiz the best place to start if you are new to map quizzes. When Click mode feels easy, switch to
    Type mode, which highlights a country and asks for its name. Learn mode drops the scoring and
    shows each country’s capital, area and population when you tap it.
  </p>

  <h2>How Many Countries Are in South America?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>South America has 12 independent countries, and the South America map quiz asks for all of
    them.</strong> Two more places are drawn but not asked. French Guiana, on the north coast between
    Suriname and Brazil, is part of France, which makes it the only part of the European Union on the
    South American mainland. The Falkland Islands in the South Atlantic are a British territory, also
    claimed by Argentina.
  </p>
  <p>
    Panama and Trinidad and Tobago sit right next to South America, but both are counted as North
    American, so they are in our <a href="/map-quiz/north-america/">North America map quiz</a>. The 12
    countries here cover about 17.7 million km² and are home to about 435 million people, and Brazil
    alone accounts for almost half of both totals.
  </p>

  <h2>Learn South America in Four Groups</h2>
  <p>
    Twelve countries do not need much organising, but four groups make the map stick faster:
  </p>
  <h3>Brazil (1)</h3>
  <p>
    Brazil covers almost half of South America, both by land and by population: about 8.5 million km²
    and 212 million people. It borders every other country on the continent except Chile and Ecuador,
    so once it is placed, the rest of the South America map quiz fits around it.
  </p>
  <h3>The north coast (3)</h3>
  <p>
    Venezuela, Guyana and Suriname, running west to east along the Caribbean and Atlantic coast, with
    French Guiana after them. Guyana’s official language is English and Suriname’s is Dutch: they are the only
    countries in South America where neither Spanish nor Portuguese is official.
  </p>
  <h3>The Andean countries (4)</h3>
  <p>
    Colombia, Ecuador, Peru and Bolivia, north to south down the Andes. Colombia is the only South
    American country with both a Pacific and a Caribbean coast. Ecuador takes its name from the
    equator, which crosses it just north of the capital, Quito.
  </p>
  <h3>The Southern Cone (4)</h3>
  <p>
    Chile, Argentina, Uruguay and Paraguay. Chile is a long ribbon down the Pacific coast, about
    4,300 km from north to south but on average less than 200 km wide. Argentina fills most of the rest of
    the south, and its border with Chile runs for more than 5,000 km along the Andes. Uruguay and
    Paraguay are the two smaller countries tucked between Argentina and Brazil.
  </p>

  <h2>The Hardest Countries in the South America Map Quiz</h2>
  <p>
    There are no microstates in South America, so the South America map quiz has no tap rings. The
    mistakes come from pairs that look alike:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Guyana and Suriname.</strong> Two similar-sized countries side by side on the north
      coast. Guyana is the western one, next to Venezuela; Suriname is the eastern one, next to French
      Guiana. Suriname is the smallest country in South America.</li>
    <li><strong>Paraguay and Uruguay.</strong> Similar names, different places. Paraguay is inland,
      north of Argentina and next to Bolivia; Uruguay is on the coast, on the Río de la Plata across
      from Buenos Aires.</li>
    <li><strong>Bolivia and Paraguay.</strong> The continent’s two landlocked countries, next to each
      other. Bolivia is the bigger one, to the north-west, high in the Andes.</li>
    <li><strong>Ecuador and Peru.</strong> Ecuador is the small country at the top of Peru, where the
      coast bends.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Memory Tricks for the Map of South America</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Start with Brazil, then go round it.</strong> Anticlockwise from the north: French
      Guiana, Suriname, Guyana, Venezuela, Colombia, Peru, Bolivia, Paraguay, Argentina and Uruguay.
      That is every neighbour in one sweep.</li>
    <li><strong>Walk down the west coast.</strong> Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Chile. Four countries and
      the whole Pacific side is done.</li>
    <li><strong>Remember the two not touching Brazil.</strong> Chile and Ecuador. Everything else in the
      South America map quiz shares a border with it.</li>
    <li><strong>Follow the Andes.</strong> The mountains run through seven countries: Venezuela,
      Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Bolivia, Chile and Argentina.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Landmarks That Fix the Map</h2>
  <p>
    A few famous places sit on or near borders, and knowing them gives the South America map quiz
    some fixed points to work from:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lake Titicaca</strong> lies on the border of Peru and Bolivia, high in the Andes.</li>
    <li><strong>The Iguazú Falls</strong> are on the border of Argentina and Brazil, close to where
      Paraguay meets them both.</li>
    <li><strong>The Río de la Plata</strong> is the wide estuary between Argentina and Uruguay, with
      Buenos Aires on one side and Montevideo on the other.</li>
    <li><strong>The Amazon</strong> flows east from Peru, past the southern tip of Colombia, and
      across northern Brazil to the Atlantic.</li>
    <li><strong>The Atacama Desert</strong> fills northern Chile, and <strong>Patagonia</strong> in the
      far south is shared by Chile and Argentina.</li>
    <li><strong>Angel Falls</strong>, the highest waterfall in the world, is in south-eastern
      Venezuela.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Some countries also own islands far off the coast. Ecuador’s Galápagos Islands lie about 900 km
    out in the Pacific, and Chile’s Easter Island is more than 3,500 km offshore. Both count as part of
    their countries, so in the South America map quiz a click on the Galápagos is a click on Ecuador.
  </p>

  <h2>Languages of South America</h2>
  <p>
    Spanish is the official language in nine of the 12 countries in the South America map quiz:
    Argentina, Bolivia, Chile, Colombia, Ecuador, Paraguay, Peru, Uruguay and Venezuela. Brazil speaks
    Portuguese, Guyana English and Suriname Dutch, while French Guiana uses French. Indigenous
    languages are official too: Guaraní alongside Spanish in Paraguay, and Quechua and Aymara in
    Peru and Bolivia, whose constitution recognises more than 30 indigenous languages.
  </p>

  <h2>South America’s Largest Countries by Area and Population</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Brazil is the largest country on the continent and has the most people, with Argentina
    second by area and Colombia second by population.</strong>
  </p>
  <table class="cmp">
    <thead><tr><th>Rank</th><th>Largest by area</th><th>Most people (2024)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1</td><td>Brazil — 8.52 million km²</td><td>Brazil — 212 million</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>Argentina — 2.78 million km²</td><td>Colombia — 53 million</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3</td><td>Peru — 1.29 million km²</td><td>Argentina — 46 million</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>Colombia — 1.14 million km²</td><td>Peru — 34 million</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5</td><td>Bolivia — 1.10 million km²</td><td>Venezuela — 28 million</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Bolivia is the fifth-largest country but has about 12 million people; Ecuador, a quarter of its
    size, has about 18 million. Try comparisons like that in
    <a href="/higher-or-lower/">Higher or Lower: Countries</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Capitals Worth Knowing</h2>
  <p>
    Learn mode shows every capital in the South America map quiz. Brazil’s capital is Brasília, a
    planned city that replaced Rio de Janeiro in 1960, not São Paulo or Rio. Bolivia splits the role:
    Sucre is the constitutional capital and La Paz the seat of government. Ecuador’s capital is Quito
    rather than its largest city, Guayaquil. The rest are the biggest cities in their countries,
    including Buenos Aires, Bogotá, Lima, Santiago and Caracas. To find them on a globe, play our
    <a href="/capitals/">capital cities game</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Using the South America Map Quiz in Class</h2>
  <p>
    Because it is short, the South America map quiz works as a warm-up at the start of a lesson. Run
    it once in Click mode, once in Type mode, and compare scores. No accounts are needed, results
    stay on each device, and Retry missed gives every student their own short list to repeat. A good
    follow-up is to have students sketch the continent from memory, label all 12 countries, and check
    their sketch against the printable answer key.
  </p>

  <h2>Printable South America Map Worksheet</h2>
  <p>
    The free <a href="/assets/printables/south-america-worksheet.svg" target="_blank"
    rel="noopener">South America worksheet</a> numbers all 12 countries from north to south, with
    blank lines alongside and a separate <a href="/assets/printables/south-america-answers.svg"
    target="_blank" rel="noopener">answer key</a>. It is laid out for A4 landscape and prints fine on
    US Letter.
  </p>

